
FIXED (2026-10-22): Surface atmospheres now condense gases above their saturation partial pressures and iterate pressure, composition, and greenhouse temperature to convergence. Negligible residual atmospheres become vacuum, including after cosmic-shoreline estimates. Stored surface temperatures also enforce phase limits; incomplete abundance lists cannot leave an anonymous noncondensing atmosphere. Warm supplied temperatures are preserved.

Original request: If surface temperature is too cold for any of the atmospheric gases that are present to stay gaseous, then those gases should be limited to at most trace amounts in the atmosphere, and in most cases to zero. If the surface temperature is too cold for any gases, then the atmospheric pressure should be zero, irrespective of cosmic shoreline. This reduction of cold atmospheres will further lower the surface pressure, so it will be important to find the values where the feedback loop converges.

FIXED (2026-10-22): New stellar planets and the HZ button use the conservative habitable zone. The orbit editor warns about close or crossing planetary orbits, and the resonance picker filters choices using the same spacing check.

Original request: From the Shift+A dialog, adding a new body around a planet defaults to a 7 day orbit and tidal locking. That's good, keep that. If adding a planet around a star, let's default it to the habitable zone. We can also show a visible warning when a planet being edited is too close to another planet's orbit, resulting in orbital instability. Make sure the ratios in the resonance picker would not be close enough to existing bodies to require this same warning.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Terrain generation, water-cloud checks, horizon temperatures, and sun-clock tooltips share a bounded local climate model with pressure-dependent heat transport, elevation cooling in meters, and warm subtropical land. Frost blends gradually across irregular spherical climate boundaries. Earth bump-map and generated-ocean checks pass.

Original request: The rocky map generator depends on a local temperature estimate to decide kinds of terrain (desert, tundra, forest, etc). There is a similar local temperature estimate for mouseover in sun clock view and current location in horizon view, however that calculation does not produce good results. Let's fix it, and if possible without degrading the map generator's performance, get the map generator using the upgraded local temperature estimate. You can use Earth and its bump map as a benchmark: desert areas will be hot, polar ice caps will be cold, etc. One thing I want to avoid is sharp straight lines between ice/water and rock in generated maps. 

FIXED (2026-10-22): WD and Sunlike filters are mutually exclusive; white-dwarf rows display stellar color swatches. Empty filtered lists disable object actions.

Original request: Add WD (for white dwarf) as an option in Stellar Neighborhood, mutually exclusive with Sunlike: selecting one deselects the other. Show each white dwarf's color in its list entry if possible.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Explicit mass and radius edits now update their measurement status and clear obsolete uncertainties. A supplied dense radius overrides the estimated-envelope prior, so the six-Earth-mass case remains rocky. The fault was stale estimated-radius metadata, not a valid high-density ice-giant classification.

Original request: Double check Planet::classify(). A planet of 6 Earth masses becomes an ice giant even if the density runs in the thousands of g/cm^3. If this is correct, explain why here.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Changing the Add Object type no longer enables star labels. The star-label picker retains its intentional enable-on-selection behavior.

Original request: Clicking certain lists and dropdowns reverts star labels back on. Only the star label list should do this.


FIXED (2026-10-22): Grayscale PNG bump maps now apply the sea-level offset before scaling to meters, removing a spurious elevation increase of about 10 km.


FIXED (2026-10-22): Extreme zoom uses consistent projection for stellar discs and flare centers, preserves tiny angular separations, scales the culling margin in pixels, and rejects offscreen disc and glare work. Regression checks cover magnification 2.5 million and distant background stars.

Original request: Zooming in on a star causes performance degradation and incorrect flare placement. Vega at magnification 2.5 million is a good example. Frame rate temporarily crawls to almost nothing (in some cases this can hang the app) and the flare is centered below and to the right of the star's disc.


FIXED (2026-10-22): Earth-view photometry regression scans 142,963 catalog stars and 4,380 galaxies at three epochs, including 3,042 variable stars. It allows known variability extrema and explicit magnitude tolerances for estimated V and integrated B photometry. All records pass.

Original request: Add a regression test that no stellar object or galactic object displays, from Earth, with a significantly brighter magnitude than its catalog vmag, allowing for variability and color band differences.


FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): PNG exports release temporary pixel storage, reject absent bump data, encode flat elevation maps without division by zero, and report buffered write errors. Repeated exports pass AddressSanitizer leak checks.

FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): Generated constellation segments now respect the documented 15-degree limit. The existing 47 Ursae Majoris and GJ 86 acceptance checks pass.

FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): Generated constellation figures reject components with fewer than three lines and allow three-star chains to close into triangles. Existing bright-star coverage and connected-shape checks pass.

FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): Favorite-star actions are disabled for an empty list, preventing selection, navigation, alerts, and removal from accessing absent entries. The application build passes.

FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): Ephemeris parsing safely handles missing, empty, short, and unordered input, closes files automatically, and retains the final sample. Interpolation uses bounded sample pairs, including both endpoint dates, and repeated loading releases prior storage. Celestial, Moon, and planet suites pass (137 checks).

FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): Procedural randomization handles absent, negative, nonfinite, and extreme physical inputs without undefined floating-to-integer conversions or union aliasing. Explicit random seeds remain unchanged; all 49 celestial checks pass.

FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): Generated constellation lines enforce half-length separation from other figures and symmetric near-miss checks, including fainter stars. The existing passerby and separation tests pass; a separate Tau Ceti shape expectation remains unresolved.

FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): UI texture loading rejects empty or oversized files, failed seeks, allocation failures, and short reads before decoding, preventing uninitialized input from reaching the image decoder. The application build passes.

FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): Reapplying exoplanet names retains heliocentric source elements instead of treating converted local inclinations as fresh observations. A regression reproduces the former orbit rotation and verifies stable positions across repeated passes. Planet and star suites pass; two older catalog checks remain separate.

FIXED (2026-10-22, code audit): The Gaia astrometry regression uses a temporary two-star catalog and explicitly loads the stellar reference table, so its distance, companion-photometry, and luminosity-class checks work without an optional external dataset. The isolated regression passes.

RESOLVED (2026-10-22): The current Tau Ceti constellation shapes are accepted. Replaced obsolete individual-line assertions with a regression checking that the Sun and Arcturus share a constellation when viewed from Tau Ceti.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Removed the object-specific inclination exception. For every host, exactly 90-degree catalog inclinations without transit evidence are treated as unknown and use the disk when available. Other values, including 89.92 degrees, remain valid. Transit metadata survives imports, caches, and saved objects; repeated catalog processing preserves inferred geometry.

OPEN (2026-10-22, code audit): The exoplanet catalog regression reports five additional objects after reloading. Reload identity handling remains unresolved.

Audit validation (2026-10-22): Application build passes. Initial full C++ run: 471 passing checks, two failing cases at that time, six skips for disabled GPU terrain/mesh paths. All 14 Python utility checks pass; shell syntax checks pass. Warning review covered all 35 application source units. This audit does not establish that all possible defects are absent.

Tau Ceti validation (2026-10-22): All 22 constellation checks pass, including the Sun–Arcturus shared-constellation regression.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Local-body roles, map inversion, known-pole status, vegetation color, and satellite center codes come from the packaged planet catalog rather than object-name conditions. Saved objects retain role metadata, older universe files import it from the catalog, and a renamed satellite host resolves by its catalog code. Celestial, satellite, and housekeeping checks pass (72 checks); application builds.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Constellation polar fallbacks use catalog boundary geometry, split regions use their catalog naming structure, and reference-vantage matching uses the reference star and its aliases. Removed object-specific star naming, import alias rewriting, and debug output. Constellation, star, and rendering suites pass (113 checks; six disabled GPU-path skips).

FIXED (2026-10-22): Removed named-object catalog branches for binary membership, host identity, source choice, planet-letter rewriting, asteroid naming, and missing radii. Host cross-identifiers and aliases come from catalog records; rejection follows source disposition; unknown asteroid radii use an albedo estimate. The legacy CSV importer also retains transit flags and converts inclination units once. Star and celestial suites pass (100 checks); 49 catalog checks pass, with the separate reload-count case still open.

FIXED (2026-10-22): Galaxy orientation, measured size and distance, band files, texture paths, and label extents are loaded from a packaged properties catalog. Removed named-galaxy rendering branches and fixed physical values from executable code. The reference star's pole is likewise read from the local catalog. A synthetic galaxy regression verifies the same data path and serialization. Application builds; the full run has 475 passing checks and the previously recorded exoplanet reload-count failure. CMake configuration could not run because CMake is absent; installation rules and installer checks include the new catalog.
